  "description": "The 50 Year Anniversary Cruiser - 24\" and 26\" Frame and Fork\nThese are 100% recreations of the Torker Cruisers made famous by the one and only, Clint \"The Barbarian\" Miller. The 26\" came first, in 1981. Then the 24\" followed in 1982. Same twin top tubes, same 4130 chromoly, same show chrome, same 1\" threaded fork.\nThe 26\" started with a dealer. Mike McLaughlin of Mike's Bikes in St. Louis kept after Steve Johnson to build a Cruiser for adults, and John Johnson backed him up. Prototyping started in the summer of 1980. Clint Miller raced the first one at the NBA Supernationals in San Diego in April 1981 and won, his first race on the big wheeler. Steve told Bicycle Motocross Action they had never had a product \"so immediately accepted as being so hot.\"\nThe 24\" was Clint's bike from the start. It began as a scaled-down 26\". Clint told Torker to shorten up the rear end and quicken the steering, and Torker built it. BMX Plus tested it in November 1982 and called it a race bike that is forgiving enough for the novice.\nFor our 50th year we are building both one more time. Welded near Fullerton, triple plated in show chrome in Santa Ana. A 100% California built bike, just down the road from where the originals came off the line.\nWhen do they arrive? They are being welded now as you read this - and Due back from Chrome the First week of December.\n26\" Cruiser - T5001C through T5025C\n24\" Small Cruiser - T5001SC through T5025SC\nCoded the way Torker did it in 1981 and 1982: \"C\" for Cruiser, \"SC\" for Small Cruiser.\nFinish: Triple plated show chrome only.\n4130 chromoly frame and fork, heli-arc welded\nTorker twin top tubes, 5/8\" O.D., running past the seat tube to the rear dropouts. Two tubes do the work of a top tube and two seat stays\n1-1/4\" O.D. down tube\n5/8\" O.D. chainstays\nGusset plate pierced by the seat tube, plus a small triangular plate between the top tubes\nMachined 4\" head tube\nRear dropouts with 1-5/8\" of axle adjustment\nLeading-axle fork with 1\" O.D. legs, drilled for a front caliper brake\n1\" threaded steerer, made the way they were in 1981\nTriple plated show chrome, plated in Santa Ana, California\nAmerican bottom bracket",
